"The acyclic hero characterization for tournaments | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/371680/the-acyclic-hero-characterization-for-tournaments)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; The acyclic hero conjecture. ... Jørgen Bang-Jensen, Lucas Picasarri-Arrieta and Anders Yeo, “Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs”, arXiv:2511.20246 (2025). ... Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n# The acyclic hero characterization for tournaments\n\n1 year old · traced to 2025\n\nLet $T$T and $H$H be tournaments. A tournament is an acyclic hero if there is a constant $c_H$c H​ such that every $H$H-free tournament has acyclic dichromatic number at most $c_H$c H​. For tournaments $H_1,H_2$H 1​,H 2​, let $H_1\\Rightarrow H_2$H 1​⇒H 2​ denote the tournament formed from disjoint copies by orienting every arc from $H_1$H 1​ to $H_2$H 2​, and let $\\Delta(H_1,H_2,H_3)$Δ(H 1​,H 2​,H 3​) denote the cyclic composition of three tournaments. Write $k$k for the transitive tournament $TT_k$T T k​. For every $k$k, define\n\n$$H_k=TT_k\\Rightarrow(\\Delta(k,1,1)\\Rightarrow TT_k),$$H k​=T T k​⇒(Δ(k,1,1)⇒T T k​),\n\nand let $\\mathcal{AH}$A H be the class of tournaments that occur as subtournaments of some $H_k$H k​.\n\nThe acyclic hero conjecture. A tournament $H$H is an acyclic hero if and only if $H\\in\\mathcal{AH}$H∈A H.\n\nThe forward implication is proved in the paper, as are several examples of tournaments in $\\mathcal{AH}$A H that are acyclic heroes. The converse, and hence the characterization, remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Jørgen Bang-Jensen, Lucas Picasarri-Arrieta and Anders Yeo, “Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs”, arXiv:2511.20246 (2025).\n\ndichromatic-numberoriented-graphstournaments\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2504.05817] Combinatorial Ricci flows on infinite disk triangulations (https://toehanger.shop/?_=%2Fabs%2F2504.05817%23KGzYqmHwYljVikqzZcqJdSc%3D)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; View a PDF of the paper titled Combinatorial Ricci flows on infinite disk triangulations, by Huabin Ge and 2 other authors ... Moreover, we prove convergence results of CRFs, which indicate a uniformization theorem for CRFs on infinite disk triangulations. ... Cite as:  | arXiv:2504.05817 [math.GT]\n\n# Mathematics > Geometric Topology\n\n[Submitted on 8 Apr 2025]\n\n# Title:Combinatorial Ricci flows on infinite disk triangulations\n\nAuthors:Huabin Ge, Bobo Hua, Puchun Zhou\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Combinatorial Ricci flows on infinite disk triangulations, by Huabin Ge and 2 other authors\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:In this paper, we introduce combinatorial Ricci flows (CRFs in short) in Euclidean and hyperbolic background geometries on infinite triangulations of the open disk, which are discrete analogs of Ricci flows on simply connected open surfaces. We establish well-posedness results, the existence and the uniqueness, of CRFs in both Euclidean and hyperbolic background geometries. Moreover, we prove convergence results of CRFs, which indicate a uniformization theorem for CRFs on infinite disk triangulations. As an application, we prove an existence result of circle-packing metrics with infinite prescribed cone angles in hyperbolic background geometry. To our knowledge, these are the first results of CRFs on infinite triangulations.\n\nComments:  | 3 figures\nSubjects:  | Geometric Topology (math.GT); Differential Geometry (math.DG)\nMSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 52C26, 51M10, 57M50\nCite as:  | arXiv:2504.05817 [math.GT]\n   | (or arXiv:2504.05817v1 [math.GT] for this version)\n   | https://do