it would extend the paper’s bounded-core separator program to a substantially broader class of graphs.\n\n### References\n\nIt provides support for the following conjecture that was posed in and seems to be gaining popularity in the community: \\begin{conjecture}\\label{conj:domsep} For every positive integer $t$, there is an integer $d=d(t)$ such that every $\\mathcal{L}_t$-free graph $G$ with no induced subgraph isomorphic to a subdivision of the $t \\times t$-wall is $d$-breakable. \\end{conjecture}\n\n— Tree independence number V. Walls and claws  (2501.14658 - Chudnovsky et al., 24 Jan 2025) in Section 1, Conjecture 2 (labelled Conjecture~\\ref{conj:domsep})\n\n### Related Problems\n\n1.\n\nPolylogarithmic tree independence for induced-minor-free graphs  Same Paper Same Paper\n\n2.\n\nBounded-core separators for wall-free graphs  Same Paper Same Paper\n\n3.\n\nPolylogarithmic tree independence for graphs excluding induced-minor walls and bicliques  Same Paper\n\n4.\n\nBounded tree-independence for {P_r, K_{2,t}}-free graphs  2604.01999\n\n5.\n\nAsymptotic gap for treedepth obstructions  2501.04166\n\n6.\n\nSidorenko’s conjecture (graph homomorphism densities)  2511.02864\n\n7.\n\nProfile and neighbourhood complexity for graphs excluding a subdivision  2501.08895\n\n8.\n\nLargest t-intersecting families for t up to n/2  2502.08128\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Tree independence number V. Walls and claws. (https://dblp.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/corr/abs-2501-14658.html)\nciteturn3search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; DOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2501.14658\n\n# \"Tree independence number V. Walls and claws.\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nMaria Chudnovsky et al. (2025)\n\n  *   * Image: use mirror server\n\nTrier\n\n  * Dagstuhl\n\n  * > Home\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n    * JSON @ openal