us statement for the ordinary dichromatic number is known. The acyclic version is proposed as a stronger local-to-global property and remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nJørgen Bang-Jensen, Lucas Picasarri-Arrieta and Anders Yeo, “Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs”, arXiv:2511.20246 (2025).\n\ndichromatic-numbertournaments\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 2 months ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReport from Dagstuhl Seminar 25041 (https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/04dagstuhl-reports/volume15/issue01/25041/DagRep.15.1.105/DagRep.15.1.105.pdf)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Walls and claws”, CoRR, Vol. abs/2501.14658, 2025.\nURL https://doi.org/10.48550/ARXIV.2501.14658\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTree-independence number VII. Excluding a star. (https://web.math.princeton.edu/~mchudnov/StarLog.pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; CoRR, abs/2501.14658, 2025.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n18  CHUDNOVSKY, GOLLIN, KRNC, AND MILANIĆ (https://web.math.princeton.edu/~mchudnov/WheelInducedMinors.pdf)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; DOI: 10.1007/978-3-032-11835-6_10  arXiv: 2506.08829 ... arXiv: 2501.14658 ... [27] Agelos Georgakopoulos and Panos Papasoglu, “Graph minors and metric spaces”, *Combinatorica*, 45(3), Paper No.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[13] Nick Brettell, Andrea Munaro, Daniël Paulusma, and Shizhou Yang. Comparing width parameters on graph clas